course: dictionary
lesson: 10122
title: Though: a word of surprise
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Though is a small word with a big job: it joins two ideas that seem not to fit together. «Though it was raining, we played outside.» Rain should keep us in, but we went out anyway. So though means «in spite of the fact that».
explain: «Even though» makes the surprise stronger: «Even though veins look blue through your skin, blood is always red.» To check a meaning, swap in «in spite of the fact that» or «however». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: At the end of a sentence, though means «however» or «but», as in «It's cold. It's sunny, though.» The second idea is a small twist on the first. Writers use it in stories to show that something unexpected happened.
fact: Though means «in spite of the fact that»; it joins two ideas that seem not to fit together.
fact: At the end of a sentence, though means «however» or «but».
fact: As though means «as if».
fact: «Even though» makes the contrast stronger than «though» on its own.
fact: A good check is to swap in «in spite of the fact that» or «however» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Though it was raining, we played outside.» What does though mean here? | Before the rain stopped | Because of the rain | *In spite of the fact that | Until it was dark
exercise: «It's cold. It's sunny, though.» What does though mean at the end of this sentence? | Because of this | *However, or but | Very quickly | Yesterday
exercise: «He smiled as though he had won the prize.» What does as though mean? | Even when | As soon as | *As if | In spite of
test: Jayden says though means the same as «so». Why is he wrong? | Though means very quickly | Though is only used at the end of a sentence | *Though joins two ideas that seem not to fit, while so shows a result | Though and so both mean the same, so he is right
test: «Though she had 10 sweets, she gave away 3.» How many sweets does she have left? | 10 | 13 | *7 | 3
exam: Which sentence uses though to show a surprise? | *«Though it snowed, the school stayed open.» | «We thought about the snow all day.» | «The snow was cold, so we went in.» | «Snow falls in winter, though.»
